By reading threads in "Lojban" (thanks for the links) I gathered the=20
following.
1. Most consider {ko broda} a neutral imperative, or at least support the=
=20
idea.
2. Lojbab confirmed that bare {ko broda} being a command is the intended=20
standard interpretation.
3. Almost everyone consider {.e'o do broda} not being a proposition, but a=
=20
close equivalent of {.e'o ko broda}.

My Thoughts

A rationale for (1) is that there are many different uses for imperatives=
=20
besides commands, and it's easier to specifically mark commands as such=20
(and only when needed) than to mark all other uses. This is also in line=20
with some other aspects of Lojban, where the shortest form leaves details=
=20
unspecified.

(2) probably came from English. In Russian, for example, I feel that bare=
=20
imperatives are appropriate in many cases. When I buy a bread that the=20
seller has to hand me, I say: "=D0=D2=CF=C4=C1=CA=D4=C5 =C8=CC=C5=C2" {ko v=
ecnu lo nanba}. This is=20
an offer of purchase, not a command. Commands in Russian often do not use=
=20
imperatives: "=F3=D4=CF=D1=D4=D8!" {nu sanli}, which may be interpreted as =
"=F0=D2=C9=CB=C1=DA=D9=D7=C1=C0=20
=D3=D4=CF=D1=D4=D8!" {mi minde fi lo nu sanli} with "=D0=D2=C9=CB=C1=DA=D9=
=D7=C1=C0" {mi minde} omitted.

If we decide in favor of (1), it will be a change to the language standard,=
=20
and we will need a good way to mark commands.

As for (3), I'd like to have a comprehensive list of words that can change=
=20
the bridi meaning from a default proposition to something else. That would=
=20
be (excluding experimental words):
questions: xu, ma, mo, xo, ji, ge'i, gi'i, gu'i, je'i, pei, fi'a, cu'e,=20
pau?;
imperatives: ko;
attitudinals, discursives, vocatives: .e'o, pe'u?, what else here?
any others kinds?
Also, is {sei mi cpedu do klama ta} a proposition or not?

--=20
